      "text": "To solve the problem, let's denote the two-digit number \\( n \\) as \\( 10a + b \\), where \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are its digits, and \\( a \\) is a non-zero digit (since \\( n \\) is a two-digit number).\n\nThe switch of the digits gives us the number \\( 10b + a \\). According to the problem, switching the digits and then adding 3 results in \\( 2n \\). Therefore, we can write the equation:\n\\[\n10b + a + 3 = 2(10a + b)\n\\]\n\nFirst, expand the right side of the equation:\n\\[\n10b + a + 3 = 20a + 2b\n\\]\n\nNext, we will isolate terms involving \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) on one side of the equation:\n\\[\n10b + a + 3 - 2b = 20a\n\\]\n\\[\n8b + a + 3 = 20a\n\\]\n\nSubtract \\( a \\) from both sides:\n\\[\n8b + 3 = 19a\n\\]\n\nWe need to find integer solutions for \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) where \\( a \\) is a digit from 1 to 9 and \\( b \\) is a digit from 0 to 9. Rearrange the equation to solve for \\( a \\):\n\\[\n19a = 8b + 3\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{8b + 3}{19}\n\\]\n\nFor \\( a \\) to be an integer, \\( 8b + 3 \\) must be divisible by 19. We will test different values of \\( b \\) to find an appropriate \\( a \\).\n\n1. If \\( b = 1 \\):\n   \\[\n   8(1) + 3 = 11\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a = \\frac{11}{19} \\quad \\text{(not an integer)}\n   \\]\n\n2. If \\( b = 2 \\):\n   \\[\n   8(2) + 3 = 19\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a = \\frac{19}{19} = 1 \\quad \\text{(integer)}\n   \\]\n   So, \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( b = 2 \\) is a solution.\n\nThus, the two-digit number \\( n \\) is:\n\\[\n10a + b = 10(1) + 2 = 12\n\\]\n\nTo verify, switch the digits of 12 to get 21. Then, add 3:\n\\[\n21 + 3 = 24\n\\]\nAnd, calculate \\( 2n \\):\n\\[\n2 \\times 12 = 24\n\\]\nThe calculations match, confirming that the smallest two-digit integer \\( n \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]",
