      "text": "Let's denote the two-digit number \\( n \\) as \\( 10a + b \\), where \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are the digits of \\( n \\). The number obtained by switching the digits is \\( 10b + a \\). According to the problem, switching the digits and then adding 3 results in \\( 2n \\). Therefore, we can write the equation:\n\n\\[ 10b + a + 3 = 2(10a + b) \\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying the right side of the equation, we get:\n\n\\[ 10b + a + 3 = 20a + 2b \\]\n\nRearranging the terms to isolate \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) on one side, we have:\n\n\\[ 10b + a + 3 - 2b - 20a = 0 \\]\n\\[ 8b - 19a + 3 = 0 \\]\n\\[ 8b = 19a - 3 \\]\n\\[ b = \\frac{19a - 3}{8} \\]\n\nSince \\( b \\) is a digit (i.e., an integer between 0 and 9), \\( 19a - 3 \\) must be divisible by 8. We need to find an integer \\( a \\) such that \\( 19a - 3 \\) is divisible by 8. Let's test the values of \\( a \\) from 1 to 9:\n\n- If \\( a = 1 \\), then \\( 19 \\cdot 1 - 3 = 16 \\), which is divisible by 8.\n- If \\( a = 2 \\), then \\( 19 \\cdot 2 - 3 = 35 \\), which is not divisible by 8.\n- If \\( a = 3 \\), then \\( 19 \\cdot 3 - 3 = 54 \\), which is not divisible by 8.\n- If \\( a = 4 \\), then \\( 19 \\cdot 4 - 3 = 73 \\), which is not divisible by 8.\n- If \\( a = 5 \\), then \\( 19 \\cdot 5 - 3 = 92 \\), which is divisible by 8.\n- If \\( a = 6 \\), then \\( 19 \\cdot 6 - 3 = 111 \\), which is not divisible by 8.\n- If \\( a = 7 \\), then \\( 19 \\cdot 7 - 3 = 130 \\), which is not divisible by 8.\n- If \\( a = 8 \\), then \\( 19 \\cdot 8 - 3 = 149 \\), which is not divisible by 8.\n- If \\( a = 9 \\), then \\( 19 \\cdot 9 - 3 = 168 \\), which is divisible by 8.\n\nThus, the possible values for \\( a \\) are 1, 5, and 9. We will now check each of these values to find the corresponding \\( b \\):\n\n1. If \\( a = 1 \\), then \\( b = \\frac{19 \\cdot 1 - 3}{8} = \\frac{16}{8} = 2 \\). So, \\( n = 10a + b = 10 \\cdot 1 + 2 = 12 \\).\n2. If \\( a = 5 \\), then \\( b = \\frac{19 \\cdot 5 - 3}{8} = \\frac{92}{8} = 11.5 \\), which is not an integer.\n3. If \\( a = 9 \\), then \\( b = \\frac{19 \\cdot 9 - 3}{8} = \\frac{168}{8} = 21 \\), which is not a digit.\n\nThe only valid solution is \\( n = 12 \\). Therefore, the smallest two-digit integer \\( n \\) such that switching its digits and then adding 3 results in \\( 2n \\) is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{12} \\]",
