      "text": "Let $n = 10a + b$ represent the two-digit number, where $a$ and $b$ are the digits, with $a$ being the tens digit and $b$ being the units digit.\n\nAfter swapping the digits, we have number $m = 10b + a$.\n\nAccording to the problem statement, switching the digits and then adding $3$ equals $2 times n$.\n\nThus, we get the equation:\n$$\n\\begin{align*}\n10b + a + 3 &= 2(10a + b) \\\\\n10b + a + 3 &= 20a + 2b \\\\\n8b - 19a &= -3\n\\end{align*}\n$$\nBy checking all pairs of $(a, b)$ where $0 < a \\le 9$ and $0 \\le b \\le 9$, we find that the smallest solution that satisfies this equation is:\n$$\n\\begin{align*}\na &= 7 \\\\\nb &= 2 \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n$$\nSo, the two-digit number $n = 10a + b = 72$.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is $\\boxed{72}$.",
